---
import CategoryLayout from '../../layouts/Category.astro';
import { getCategory } from '../../data/tools';

const category = getCategory('generate');
---

<CategoryLayout category={category}>
  <section class="section">
    <h2>One source image, a complete set out</h2>
    <p>
      These tools exist because the work is repetitive rather than difficult. A website needs
      eight icon files in specific sizes with a manifest and the right HTML. A product launch
      needs the same photograph at a dozen platform sizes. Doing that by hand in an image editor
      is an afternoon; doing it here is a click.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/favicon-generator/">Favicon generator</a> — every icon size a modern site needs,
        a true multi-resolution <code>favicon.ico</code>, the web manifest, and the HTML to paste
        into your head.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/social-media-image-sizes/">Social media sizes</a> — one image resized correctly
        for Instagram, YouTube, TikTok, Pinterest, Etsy, Amazon and more, bundled into a ZIP.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/add-watermark/">Watermark</a> — a text or logo mark applied to one image or a
        whole batch with consistent placement.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Start from the largest original you have</h2>
    <p>
      Everything here derives outputs from your source, so the source sets the quality ceiling for
      all of them. Scaling down looks excellent; scaling up cannot invent detail that was never
      captured.
    </p>
    <p>
      The largest targets are 3840 pixels for a YouTube thumbnail, 2048 for a Shopify product
      image and 2000 for an Etsy listing, so a source around 2000 pixels on its shortest side
      covers nearly everything. Both generators warn you when a requested output is larger than
      your source rather than quietly producing something soft.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Batch work and consistency</h2>
    <p>
      The watermark tool applies one configuration across every file you drop in, which is the
      point — a batch of listing photos with the mark in a different place on each looks
      careless.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things to check before committing to a large batch. Preview against your brightest and
      darkest images, because a single opacity setting has to work for both. And confirm the
      platform allows watermarks at all: Amazon prohibits them on main product images, and a
      suppressed listing is a worse outcome than an unwatermarked photo.
    </p>
  </section>
</CategoryLayout>
